WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.819 --> 00:00:05.640
Proteins play countless roles throughout the
biological world.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.059
Some transport nutrients throughout the body.

00:00:09.059 --> 00:00:12.360
Some help the chemical reactions happen at
a faster rate.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.049
Others build the structures of the living
things.

00:00:15.049 --> 00:00:19.340
Despite this wide range of functions, all
proteins are made out of the same 21 building

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.890
blocks, called amino acids.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:27.970
Amino acids are made of carbon, oxygen, nitrogen,
and hydrogen, and some contain sulfur atoms.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:35.010
Selenocysteine is the only standard amino
acid that contains a selenium atom.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:39.940
These atoms form an amino group, a carboxyl
group, and a side chain attached to a central

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.320
carbon atom.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:49.879
The side chain is the only part that varies
from amino acid to amino acid and determines

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:52.789
its properties.

00:00:52.789 --> 00:00:57.719
Hydrophobic amino acids have carbon-rich side
chains, which don’t interact well with water.

00:00:57.719 --> 00:01:05.780
Hydrophilic or polar amino acids interact
well with water.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:19.390
Charged amino acids interact with oppositely
charged amino acids or other molecules.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:24.260
Primary Structure
The primary structure of a protein is the

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:30.550
linear sequence of amino acids as encoded
by DNA.

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:34.810
The amino acids in a protein are joined by
peptide bonds, which link the amino group

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.740
of one amino acid to the carboxyl group of
another.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:42.119
A water molecule is released each time a bond
is formed.

00:01:42.119 --> 00:01:47.200
The linked series of carbon, nitrogen, and
oxygen atoms make up the protein backbone.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:53.600
These protein chains often fold into two types
of secondary structures: alpha helices, or

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.560
beta sheets.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.680
An alpha helix is a right-handed coil stabilized
by hydrogen bonds between the amine and carboxyl

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:05.039
groups of nearby amino acids.

00:02:05.039 --> 00:02:10.570
Beta-sheets are formed when hydrogen bonds
stabilize two or more adjacent strands.

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:16.750
The tertiary structure of a protein is the
three-dimensional shape of the protein chain.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:22.660
This shape is determined by the characteristics
of the amino acids making up the chain.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:27.521
Many proteins form globular shapes with hydrophobic
side chains sheltered inside, away from the

00:02:27.521 --> 00:02:31.890
surrounding water.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:37.250
Membrane-bound proteins have hydrophobic residues
clustered together on the outside, so that

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:42.330
they can interact with the lipids in the membrane.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:50.150
Charged amino acids allow proteins to interact
with molecules that have complementary charges.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:57.510
The functions of many proteins rely on their
three-dimensional shapes.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:02.591
For example, hemoglobin forms a pocket to
hold heme, a small molecule with an iron atom

00:03:02.591 --> 00:03:08.900
in the center that binds oxygen.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.170
Two or more polypeptide chains can come together
to form one functional molecule with several

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.170
subunits.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:19.310
The four subunits of hemoglobin cooperate
so that the complex can pick up more oxygen

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:23.670
in the lungs and release it in the body.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:27.701
Different visual representations of proteins
can give us visual clues about the protein

00:03:27.701 --> 00:03:29.830
structure and function.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:34.740
This space filling diagram shows all atoms
that are making up this protein.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:40.360
This representation, called ribbon or cartoon
diagram shows the organization of the protein

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.540
backbone and highlights the alpha helices.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:51.230
This surface representation shows the areas
that are accessible to water molecules.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:54.160
Most proteins are smaller than the wavelength
of light.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.900
For example, the hemoglobin molecule is about
6.5 nanometers in size.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.100
Hemoglobin is found in high concentration
in red blood cells.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:11.100
A typical red blood cell contains about 280
million hemoglobin molecules.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:24.300
The three-dimensional shapes of proteins determine
their function.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:30.350
The flexible arms of antibodies protect us
from disease by recognizing and binding to

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:34.400
pathogens and targeting them for destruction
by the immune system.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.400
The hormone insulin is a small, stable protein
that can easily maintain its shape while traveling

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.500
through the blood to regulate the blood glucose
level.

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:12.500
Alpha Amylase is an enzyme that begins digestion
of starches in our saliva.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:23.900
The calcium pump is aided by magnesium and powered by ATP

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.800
to move calcium ions back to the sarcoplasmic reticulum after each muscle contraction.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:44.880
Ferritin is a spherical protein with channels
that allow the iron atoms to enter and exit

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.320
depending upon organism’s needs.

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:51.710
On the inside ferritin forms a hollow space
interior with atoms attached to the inner

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:52.500
wall.

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:57.500
Ferritin stores iron in the non-toxic form.

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:11.000
Collagen forms a strong triple helix that
is used throughout the body for structural support

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:24.420
Collagen molecules can form elongated fibrils
which aggregate to form collagen fibers.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.000
This type of collagen is found in skin and
tendons.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.690
Learn more about the functions and 3D structures
of proteins and other molecular machines at

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:36.900
the RCSB Protein Data Bank.

